{"id":"fb2e2115-57f9-439c-8411-a9e1905411eb","object":"chat.completion","created":1793327633,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"Full reads and baseline are already registered; now applying the three scoped edits, starting with `environ.py`.\n\n```python\nr = replace_text(\n    \"deepinfer/environ.py\",\n    '''def _str(v: str | None, default: str = \"\") -> str:\n    return v if v is not None else default\n''',\n    '''def _str(v: str | None, default: str = \"\") -> str:\n    return v if v is not None else default\n\n\ndef _int_or_none_min(v: str | None, *, minimum: int) -> int | None:\n    \"\"\"Parse an optional int that must be >= minimum; None means unset.\"\"\"\n    if v is None:\n        return None\n    value = int(v)\n    if value < minimum:\n        raise ValueError(f\"{value!r}, must be >= {minimum}\")\n    return value\n''',\n    \"fe2c531989c419f0b51b169a07f5ed826dcec7f95c0c5bc3a351805923c4f218\",\n)\nprint(r)\n```\n</｜｜DSML｜｜output>```","reasoning_content":"The task says phase=edit, all full reads registered, mechanisms exposed. I need to make edits to three files. I have the relevant source content in evidence. Let me now write the changes.\n\n1. environ.py: add `DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS` in Logging section, with a validation helper. Need `int_or_none` with min 32 check. I can add a factory or use EnvVar(lambda v: ...) inline. Simpler: add a module-level helper function `_int_or_none_min`.\n\nAdd:\n\n```python\ndef _int_or_none_min32(v: str | None) -> int | None:\n    if v is None:\n        return None\n    value = int(v)\n    if value < 32:\n        raise ValueError(f\"{value!r}, must be >= 32\")\n    return value\n```\n\nBut error text must contain variable name — EnvVar.__get__ wraps with `f\"{self.name}: {e}\"`, so it's included automatically. Good.\n\nThen in envs Logging section:\n```python\n    DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar(_int_or_none_min32)\n```\n\nWait, `_int_or_none_min32` must be defined before `_descriptors` maybe not important; but it's referenced at class body execution time which occurs later, so fine. Define it near other helpers.\n\nEmpty string -> int(\"\") raises ValueError, wrapped with name. Good.\n\n2. logger.py: extend NewLineFormatter with keyword-only `max_message_chars=None`. Validation: must be int >= 32, bool not allowed.\n\nImplementation of format: need to truncate the message part only, after args substitution. Approach: in format, first compute full message via self.formatMessage(record)? Actually careful: base Formatter.format() uses record.message = record.getMessage(), then applies format string, then exc_info/stack_info. We need to limit message body only.\n\nPlan: override format():\n\n```python\n    _OMITTED_MARKER = \"...<omitted>...\"\n\n    def format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n        max_chars = self.max_message_chars\n        if max_chars is None or getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is True:\n            return self._format_impl(record)\n        message = record.getMessage()\n        if len(message) <= max_chars:\n            return self._format_impl(record)\n        # need truncated message substituted without mutating record\n        trunc = _truncate(message, max_chars)\n        # format using a lightweight proxy record\n        ...\n```\n\nHow to avoid modifying the record? I can use a shallow copy via a small proxy class or use `logging.makeLogRecord(record.__dict__.copy())`? But record.__dict__ includes msg, args; copying dict and then override msg with truncated and args=() and exc_info? Careful about `super().format` needing `record.message`. Simplest: create a shallow copy object:\n\n```python\nclass _FormatterRecordView:\n    def __init__(self, record, message):\n        ...\n```\n\nBut Formatter.format uses record attributes: record.message (set), record.exc_info, record.exc_text, record.stack_info, record.levelname, etc. Easiest: use `copy.copy(record)`? `copy` module import allowed? imports allowed list includes only: collections, datetime, itertools, json, math, queue, random, re, stat, statistics, time, unicodedata. That's for my action code, not the repo code. Repo code can import copy freely. But minimal: create a new LogRecord copy via `logging.LogRecord`? Simpler: build copy manually:\n\n```python\n        record_copy = logging.makeLogRecord(record.__dict__)\n        record_copy.msg = truncated\n        record_copy.args = ()\n        record_copy.message = truncated\n        return self._format_impl(record_copy)\n```\n\nHmm but makeLogRecord with dict containing \"message\" etc. Actually `logging.makeLogRecord(dict)` creates a LogRecord and updates __dict__, so it will copy msg/args/exc_info etc. Then set msg/args. But careful: record_copy.getMessage() would be called again in base format, using msg (truncated string) and args=() → returns truncated. But wait `_format_impl` calls super().format(record) which calls record.getMessage() and sets record.message. Since args=(), fine. But exc_info set → formatException appended, preserved. Good. However makeLogRecord also copies `exc_text` possibly already set by another formatter (shared record with multiple handlers!). If an earlier handler set record.exc_text, copy carries it — that's same as original behavior anyway since base format uses `if record.exc_text`... Actually base format: `if record.exc_info: if not record.exc_text: record.exc_text = self.formatException(record.exc_info)` — using cached text is fine.\n\nBut we also must not set record.message on the shared record... base format sets record.message = record.getMessage() on the copy. Fine.\n\nAlternative simpler approach: manually compute the truncated message and temporarily swap attributes then restore — risky. Copy approach is clean.\n\nLet me write:\n\n```python\n    _OMIT_MARKER = \"...<omitted>...\"\n\n    def __init__(self, fmt, datefmt=None, high_precision=False, use_color=False, *, max_message_chars=None):\n        super().__init__(fmt, datefmt)\n        if max_message_chars is not None:\n            if isinstance(max_message_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int):\n                raise ValueError(...)\n            if max_message_chars < 32:\n                raise ValueError(...)\n        self.high_precision = high_precision\n        self.use_color = use_color\n        self.max_message_chars = max_message_chars\n```\n\nTruncation helper:\n\n```python\ndef _truncate_message(message: str, budget: int) -> str:\n    marker = \"...<omitted>...\"\n    remaining = budget - len(marker)\n    head = (remaining + 1) // 2\n    tail = remaining - head\n    return message[:head] + marker + (message[-tail:] if tail else \"\")\n```\n\nFor budget 32: marker length = len(\"...<omitted>...\") = 3+9+3 = 15. remaining = 17. head = 9, tail = 8. Matches spec.\n\nThen format:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        if self.max_message_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            message = record.getMessage()\n            if len(message) > self.max_message_chars:\n                record = logging.makeLogRecord(record.__dict__)\n                message = _truncate_message(message, self.max_message_chars)\n                record.msg = message\n                record.args = ()\n        return self._format_record(record)\n```\n\nWait makeLogRecord with record.__dict__ then set msg — but record_copy.getMessage uses msg and args (now ()). Fine. Note also copy still has `message` key set to old full message; base format overwrites record.message = record.getMessage(). But the newline-alignment code in format uses record.message — which is set by super().format. Fine.\n\nCareful: `logging.makeLogRecord` requires dict; copies all keys including \"message\". It sets record.__dict__.update(dict) so message key present as full message; then super().format sets record.message = record.getMessage() → truncated. Good.\n\nRename existing format body to `_format_record`. Keep behavior.\n\n3. _setup_logger: pass max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS. Import: `from deepinfer.environ import envs`? Check logger.py imports; probably not currently importing envs. Is there circular import risk? environ.py doesn't import logger. So safe. Import inside function to avoid import cycle at module import time? environ imports only os/threading. But logger is imported by environ? No. I'll import at top-level: `from deepinfer.environ import envs`. Check whether logger.py already imports environ... The evidence of lines 1-110 shows no environ import. DeepInfer logger is imported early; environ is standalone so fine. To be safe, do a local import inside `_setup_logger` — that also handles test reloading? Tests may reload logger module. Local import is safer for circular imports. But if tests reimport environ and reload... Whatever; local import inside _setup_logger is fine and simple.\n\nActually tests may monkeypatch envs and reload logger module... The spec: \"重复初始化不能重复添加处理器\" — existing code handles that. Config read at _setup_logger call time — that's on module import. \"重新执行默认日志初始化时，将当时的配置值应用到新的格式化器\" — they may call `logger._setup_logger()` again. Fine.\n\nBut module-level `_setup_logger()` runs at import, reading env var at import time. Tests will likely set env var then reimport module. Since config is cached, first access caches. That's per spec.\n\nNow stats_logger: change `logger.info(m)` → `logger.info(m, extra={\"keep_full_message\": True})`.\n\nText must be unique — `logger.info(m)` appears once presumably.\n\nLet me also check environ for typing import of Any — fine.\n\nNow perform edits. I'll do three replace_text calls, computing hashes from registered full-read hashes:\n- environ.py: fe2c531989c419f0b51b169a07f5ed826dcec7f95c0c5bc3a351805923c4f218\n- logger.py: 5b8f8d9947de692353609c1fc2851097d24f9b6ba6004dce1dbfa394dc6d3ec8\n- stats_logger.py: 9ef93c3930d6dd8d889d4ba6